The six Painlevé equations P_I, P_II, …, P_VI are a distinguished list of second-order nonlinear ordinary differential equations in the complex plane whose general solutions cannot be expressed in terms of previously known special functions; these general solutions are called Painlevé transcendents. Each equation possesses a rich variety of analytically inequivalent families of solutions, including:

(i) general two-parameter transcendental solutions;
(ii) one-parameter families of classical "special-function" solutions expressible in terms of hypergeometric, Bessel, Airy, Weber, Whittaker, or Kummer functions, occurring for special values of the parameters;
(iii) rational and algebraic solutions occurring for special parameter values;
(iv) distinguished pole-free or sectorially pole-free solutions, such as the Ablowitz–Segur family, the Hastings–McLeod solution of P_II, the tronquée solutions (free of poles in a sector of opening 4π/(n−2) for P_I-type equations), and the tri-tronquée solutions (free of poles in a sector of opening larger than that, characterized uniquely up to symmetries).

Problem: Devise a standardized, systematic notation for solutions of each of the Painlevé equations P_I–P_VI that simultaneously
(a) identifies the underlying equation and its parameter values;
(b) distinguishes the analytic class of solution (general transcendental, special-function, rational/algebraic, tronquée, tri-tronquée, Hastings–McLeod, Ablowitz–Segur, etc.);
(c) specifies the free parameters or boundary/asymptotic conditions selecting the particular solution within its class;
(d) is compatible with the Bäcklund transformation/symmetry group action on solutions, so that transformed solutions can be denoted in a coherent way;
(e) is concise enough to be used uniformly across analytical, asymptotic, and numerical studies.

Construct such a notation and verify that it covers all currently identified analytically distinct families of Painlevé transcendents.

